WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:05.250
While creator Matt Groening is best known
for another little animated series he started

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:11.240
working on in the '80s, many fans would argue
that "Futurama" is his true magnum opus.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:17.080
Here are some of the darkest "Futurama" fan
theories that you'll never be able to unlearn.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:21.690
In "Parasites Lost," the fourth episode of
Season 3, Fry's legendary appetite gets the

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:22.770
better of him.

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:26.390
Despite Leela's objections, the dimwitted
delivery boy gets down with a questionable

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:31.500
sandwich from a space gas station, and predictably,
he finds his insides crawling with parasitic

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:32.500
worms.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:37.519
Fortunately for Fry, these worms aren't concerned
with causing indigestion and diarrhea; instead,

00:00:37.519 --> 00:00:41.890
they immediately get to work establishing
a society and improving their host body.

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:46.589
"Of all the parasites I've had over the years,
these worms are among the...oh, hell, they

00:00:46.589 --> 00:00:47.749
are the best!"

00:00:47.749 --> 00:00:52.819
The worms tone Fry's muscles, turning him
into a shredded god, and wire new synaptic

00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:54.679
connections in his brain.

00:00:54.679 --> 00:00:58.859
Their work bestows the lowly delivery boy
with super intelligence and the ability to

00:00:58.859 --> 00:01:03.519
play the maddeningly difficult holophonor
— a musical instrument that projects holographic

00:01:03.519 --> 00:01:05.660
images when expertly played.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:10.000
The new Fry even catches Leela's eye, though
he ultimately becomes concerned that Leela

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.390
only loves him for his parasites.

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:16.800
In true "Futurama" fashion, Fry is cured of
his helpful parasites by the end of the episode,

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.860
but one fan theory suggests this wasn't the
end of the story — at least, not for Fry.

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:26.290
According to this dark theory, the parasites
left a hole in Fry's soul — the understanding

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:27.790
of his true potential.

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:31.860
Proponents of this theory argue that you can
see a marked change in Fry's behavior from

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:33.790
this point in the series on.

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:38.580
Forever haunted by the brief glimpse of personal
success that the parasites offered, Fry becomes

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:44.200
less irresponsible and more committed to self-improvement
to make himself once again worthy of Leela.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:47.450
The last scene of the episode lends some credence
to this theory.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:52.020
In that moment, Fry is seen sitting by himself
with a holophonor, attempting a rudimentary

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:55.020
melody that projects a sloppy portrait of
Leela.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:02:00.030
It's a sad story, since Fry never again achieves
the heights of success afforded him by the

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:01.460
worms.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:05.820
If you've only seen a few episodes of "Futurama"
here and there, you might have a hard time

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:10.869
imagining that Bender Bending Rodriguez was
ever anything other than the foul-mouthed,

00:02:10.869 --> 00:02:14.609
beer-swilling, flatulent robot that we've
all come to know and love.

00:02:14.609 --> 00:02:20.749
But what if that roguish personality on display
for 139 of the show's 140 episodes was nothing

00:02:20.749 --> 00:02:22.700
more than a run time error?

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:27.650
According to one disturbing theory, Bender
just wasn't the same robot before his happenstance

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:32.469
meeting outside the suicide booth with Philip
J. Fry in the show's very first episode.

00:02:32.469 --> 00:02:35.920
When the two future roommates first met in
the pilot, they were both on the verge of

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:39.180
dying — Bender by intention, and Fry by
mistake.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:43.680
We learn in the course of their meet-cute
that Bender used to help make suicide booths

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:46.959
by bending the metal girders used in their
construction.

00:02:46.959 --> 00:02:51.389
This sad, blue collar bending robot willing
to take his own life out of shame for the

00:02:51.389 --> 00:02:55.900
moral repugnance of his profession is a bit
of a departure from the Bender who signs up

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:58.299
to crew Professor Farnsworth's delivery ship.

00:02:58.299 --> 00:02:59.800
So, what changed?

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:04.099
According to one fan theory, the big shift
happened just moments later, when Bender is

00:03:04.099 --> 00:03:06.860
electrocuted in The Head Museum's Hall of
Criminals.

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:12.389
After that shock, Bender seems much more willing
to bend the rules, pun intended, and go along

00:03:12.389 --> 00:03:15.669
with Fry's escape plan while they run from
the cops.

00:03:15.669 --> 00:03:20.969
The theory suggests that this one shock was
enough to reboot Bender's entire personality.

00:03:20.969 --> 00:03:25.139
We actually see a version of this play out
in a later episode, when Bender is rebooted

00:03:25.139 --> 00:03:28.180
as a result of trauma from a killer whale
attack.

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:32.669
That reboot makes Bender act like a penguin,
suggesting that his surroundings at the time

00:03:32.669 --> 00:03:37.059
of reboot actually do impact his personality
algorithm.

00:03:37.059 --> 00:03:41.480
Zapp Brannigan is one of the most memorable
secondary characters on "Futurama."

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:45.999
When the bumbling starship captain was first
introduced, he was little more than a one-dimensional,

00:03:45.999 --> 00:03:50.700
self-aggrandizing buffoon — a satire of
characters like William Shatner's James Kirk,

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:52.959
original captain of the Starship Enterprise.

00:03:52.959 --> 00:03:56.949
That all changes on subsequent episodes, however,
as the character accrues significant depth

00:03:56.949 --> 00:04:00.909
across several intersecting missions with
the Planet Express crew.

00:04:00.909 --> 00:04:05.549
He gains so much depth, in fact, that some
fans have theorized that some of his behavior

00:04:05.549 --> 00:04:07.840
may be explained by PTSD.

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:13.409
This dark theory weaves together several disparate
pieces of Brannigan's slowly unfolding backstory

00:04:13.409 --> 00:04:15.049
into a tapestry of woe.

00:04:15.049 --> 00:04:19.220
The canonical "Futurama" comics help explain
some things, by establishing that Brannigan

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:24.470
was the greatest military recruit the Democratic
Order of Planets had ever seen.

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:29.310
He was so gifted, in fact, that scientists
hoped to make him into an army by cloning

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:30.310
him.

00:04:30.310 --> 00:04:34.480
The experiment was botched, leaving Brannigan
as a sex-crazed, junk food-craving, diminished

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:36.420
version of his former self.

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:40.690
His early successes help explain why he was
able to rise through the ranks despite the

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:44.830
experiment gone wrong, but then things seem
to take a darker turn.

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:49.730
According to Zapp himself, his crowning military
achievement came when he vanquished the Killbots

00:04:49.730 --> 00:04:54.340
by making the decision to send every single
one of his soldiers into battle simultaneously,

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:57.080
thus overwhelming the Killbots' programming.

00:04:57.080 --> 00:05:01.940
Such a gutsy surge tactic likely came with
substantial human casualties, a cost that

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:05.230
this theory suggests has haunted Zapp ever
since.

00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:09.950
Tortured by the post-traumatic stress of losing
so many men, Brannigan's already self-destructive

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:11.820
tendencies only got worse.

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:17.540
So, we got a formerly great soldier who became
a formerly great captain, a shell of his former

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:21.980
self more concerned with women and food than
he is with service.

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:26.460
It's hard to laugh at a serious psychological
disorder like PTSD, so we really hope this

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:30.730
theory isn't true, but we'd be lying if we
said it doesn't make a lot of sense.

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:35.260
Of all the Futurama fan theories out there
in the multiverse, this one is definitely

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:36.630
the darkest.

